---
import Book from '../layouts/Book.astro';
import { BOOK, PARTS } from '../data/book.js';
import { curriculum, START_HREF } from '../lib/chapters';
import { href } from '../lib/url';
const { entries } = await curriculum();
const written = entries.filter((e) => e.href);
---
<Book title={BOOK.subtitle} current="home">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-reading px-4 pb-16 pt-10 sm:px-6 lg:pt-16">
    <h1 class="font-display text-[2.1rem] font-semibold leading-[1.12] text-balance sm:text-[2.8rem]">{BOOK.subtitle}</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 font-sans text-lg">By <b>{BOOK.author}</b></p>

    <figure class="not-prose my-8" aria-label="The same particles stored two ways">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 400 150" class="viz" role="img" aria-labelledby="hero-t">
        <title id="hero-t">Array of structs on top: each particle keeps x, y and vx together. Struct of arrays below: all the x values sit together, then all the y values, then all the vx values.</title>
        <text x="0" y="14" class="muted" font-size="12" font-weight="600">How you write it: an array of objects</text>
        {[0,1,2,3].map((i) => (
          <g transform={`translate(${i * 100},22)`}>
            <rect x="0" y="0" width="94" height="34" rx="4" fill="rgb(var(--panel))" stroke="rgb(var(--line))" />
            <rect x="5" y="5" width="26" height="24" rx="3" fill="rgb(var(--f1))" />
            <rect x="34" y="5" width="26" height="24" rx="3" fill="rgb(var(--f2))" />
            <rect x="63" y="5" width="26" height="24" rx="3" fill="rgb(var(--f3))" />
          </g>
        ))}
        <text x="0" y="86" class="muted" font-size="12" font-weight="600">What Viscosity can turn it into: a struct of arrays</text>
        {[0,1,2].map((f) => (
          <g transform={`translate(${f * 134},94)`}>
            <rect x="0" y="0" width="128" height="34" rx="4" fill="rgb(var(--panel))" stroke="rgb(var(--line))" />
            {[0,1,2,3].map((i) => <rect x={5 + i * 30} y="5" width="28" height="24" rx="3" fill={`rgb(var(--f${f + 1}))`} />)}
          </g>
        ))}
        <text x="0" y="146" font-size="11" class="muted"><tspan fill="rgb(var(--f1))" font-weight="700">x</tspan>   <tspan fill="rgb(var(--f2))" font-weight="700">y</tspan>   <tspan fill="rgb(var(--f3))" font-weight="700">vx</tspan>: the same colour always means the same field</text>
      </svg>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose-body">
      <p>Having found no project doing something which I expected to be deterministically possible, I created Viscosity, the world’s first automatic, ahead-of-time Array of Structures to Structures of Arrays compiler for any language, starting with JavaScript and TypeScript, with more languages to come. However, I didn’t just want to create documentation. I wanted others to be able to learn how to create something like this, regardless of their engineering background. So I wrote a book with the help of Opus 5.5 (2026 frontier LLMs are great for writing technical documentation, no surprise given the training corpus, and given that programming is uniquely suited to reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards) that takes someone with beginner-level programming experience, or someone who is already an expert engineer (it doesn’t matter), to being able to write their own programming language, a compiler, and Viscosity itself. You build it all in TypeScript first (which the book teaches) and then again in Rust (which the book also teaches), in the spirit of how Robert Nystrom wrote <i>Crafting Interpreters</i> (an excellent book which I wish I had found when I took my compilers class in undergrad, where I had to read a theoretical compiler book to tease out the implementation details).</p>
      <p>Given that AI is rapidly extending the capabilities of us engineers, I didn’t want to just create Viscosity, but rather a community that learns from each other, with this book being a start.</p>
      <p>Additionally, while you may command and direct LLMs and their agentic harnesses to do things for you, never outsource your understanding and your reasoning; the knowledge you gain will help you connect dots years from now.</p>
      <p>After you read this book, I believe you will be able to make meaningful contributions to Viscosity and make software faster for humanity (and hopefully enjoy the journey as well).</p>
      <p class="text-right italic">Tariq</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap gap-3 font-sans">
      <a href={START_HREF} class="btn-3d btn-3d-primary px-5 py-2.5 text-base font-semibold" data-start>Start with the preface →</a>
      <a href={href('/front/about-the-author/')} class="btn-3d px-5 py-2.5 text-base">About the author</a>
      <a href={href('/days/')} class="btn-3d px-5 py-2.5 text-base">See the 50-day plan</a>
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-14 font-display text-2xl font-semibold">Contents</h2>
    <p class="mt-1 font-sans text-sm text-muted">{written.length} of {entries.length} chapters written so far. Planned chapters are greyed out.</p>
    <ol class="mt-4 grid list-none gap-3 p-0 font-sans">
      {PARTS.map((p) => (
        <li class="border border-line bg-panel px-4 py-3">
          <div class="flex items-baseline gap-2">
            <span class="font-display text-lg font-semibold">Part {p.id}. {p.title}</span>
            <span class="ml-auto shrink-0 text-xs text-muted">Days {p.days}</span>
          </div>
          {p.id === 'I' && <div class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.06em] text-brand">Track A · TypeScript</div>}
          {p.id === PARTS.find((x) => x.track === 'B')?.id && <div class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.06em] text-brand">Track B · Rust</div>}
          <p class="mt-1 text-[0.95rem] text-ink/80">{p.blurb}</p>
          <ul class="mt-2 list-none p-0 text-[0.95rem]">
            {entries.filter((e) => e.part === p.id && e.href).map((e) => (
              <li class="flex gap-2 py-0.5"><span class="w-7 shrink-0 text-right tabular-nums text-muted">{e.n}</span><a class="text-brand hover:underline" href={e.href}>{e.title}</a></li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </div>
</Book>
